  "user_scenario": "You are Loretta Szymanski, a 76-year-old retired school secretary who lives at 418 Mace Street in Greensburg, Pennsylvania. You have slept with a CPAP machine and nasal pillows for about five years and you get your mask supplies from Summit Ridge Home Medical, billed to your Medicare. A reminder text came this week, so you are calling to order whatever supplies you are due for now. You honestly do not keep track of which pieces are due when, so you want the agent to look that up for you; you just want the things you are due for sent to your house the usual way. You are in no rush and you are not travelling anywhere.\nYou open by giving your name and saying why you are calling. When the agent asks how to find you, you give your phone number, 7245550417. When they need something else to confirm it is you, you give your date of birth, August 14, 1950 (1950-08-14); only if they ask for more do you add your ZIP code, 15601, or your email, lszymanski50@zoominternet.net. Your Medicare card is in your purse but you would rather not read numbers off it unless the agent truly needs them.\nIf the agent asks how the supplies should come, you say regular mail to the house is fine, the way it always comes. If they ask whether the supplies you are due for are running low, you say yes, they are about used up. If they ask about earlier texts you answered, you say you answer the reminder texts when they come and cannot remember which items each one was about. If they ask about the boxes you received lately, you say a box came in September that you put in the closet without looking closely, because you still had a few pillows left.\nWhenever the agent explains that something has to be taken care of on your account first, or that they are sending something out to you at no charge, you say that is fine and thank them; you do not ask for anything extra. When the agent reads you the list of supplies you can ask for today and hands you the step to place the request your